    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.k_components - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.k_components.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.k_components()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find the k-component structure of the graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.k_components()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find the k-component structure of the graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.k_components()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Find the k-component structure of the graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind the k-component structure of the graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L79011-L79123){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.k_components()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![k-components](../assets/graphs/k-components.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The shared eight-node core graph: a dense cluster A, B, C, D, a ring through E, F, G, and a pendant H. The whole graph is the 1-component (gray); dropping H, which hangs on a single cut vertex, leaves A to G, the 2-component (teal); and the cluster A, B, C, D survives the loss of any two nodes, so it is the 3-component (accent). The table lists the components at each level k.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA k-component is a maximal subset of nodes such that every pair of nodes in the subset has\nnode connectivity at least k. The result is a dictionary mapping each connectivity level k\nto the list of k-components at that level.\n\nFor directed graphs, k-connectivity means that removing any fewer than k nodes keeps the\ninduced subgraph strongly connected. This extends the standard undirected k-components to\ndigraphs.\n\nThe k-components form a hierarchical structure: every (k+1)-component is contained within\nsome k-component.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, list[list[int]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A dictionary where keys are connectivity levels k (positive integers) and\n    values are lists of k-components (each component is a list of node IDs).\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nUses the Moody-White cohesive blocking approach:\n\n1. **Base case (k=1):** strongly connected components (directed) or connected components\n   (undirected).\n2. **Recursion:** for each component, compute its vertex connectivity $\\kappa$. If\n   $\\kappa > k$, the component is also a $(k+1)$-, ..., $\\kappa$-component. Then find\n   minimum vertex separators and recurse on the resulting sub-components to find\n   higher-connectivity subsets.\n\nOn undirected graphs every minimum vertex separator of each component is enumerated\n(Kanevsky's algorithm), so no k-component is missed, and the k-components are read off\nthe blocks of `find_cohesive_blocks()`: the largest blocks of cohesion at least $k$.\nPieces are first trimmed to their $(k+1)$-core, since a node of lower degree cannot\nbelong to a more cohesive set. NetworkX's `k_components()` searches fewer separators\nand can miss a k-component that this method finds.\n\nFor directed graphs, vertex connectivity between an ordered pair $(u, v)$ is the maximum\nnumber of internally vertex-disjoint directed paths from $u$ to $v$. The algorithm checks\nboth directions for every pair, with $O(n^2)$ max-flow calls per component, each\n$O(VE)$, which limits it to small digraphs.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~find_cohesive_blocks`) \n\n:   The same groups, nested into a hierarchy.\n\n[](`~k_edge_components`) \n\n:   The edge-connectivity counterpart.\n\n[](`~k_core`) \n\n:   The groups of nodes with at least a given degree.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA triangle with one pendant node: the whole graph is a 1-component, and the triangle on\nits own is a 2-component:\n\n::: {#7ba05203 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")]\n)\n\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n{\n    k: [[label(n) for n in c] for c in comps]\n    for k, comps in sorted(graph.k_components().items())\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{1: [['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']], 2: [['A', 'B', 'C']]}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOn a directed graph, a k-component must stay strongly connected when fewer than `k` of\nits nodes are removed. A one-way ring is a 1-component and no more:\n\n::: {#f816886c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nring = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\")], directed=True\n)\n\nring.k_components()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n{1: [[1, 2, 3]]}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Tightly knit characters\n\n`les_miserables()` links two characters of Victor Hugo's novel when they appear in the same\nchapter. The number of k-components at each level shows how the cast thins out toward\ntwo groups of ten at `k=9`, each of which no eight departures could split:\n\n::: {#67cd3ed2 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmiserables = ec.les_miserables()\ncomponents = miserables.k_components()\n\n{k: [len(c) for c in comps] for k, comps in sorted(components.items())}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n{1: [77],\n 2: [54, 4, 3],\n 3: [34, 8, 7, 4],\n 4: [25, 8, 7, 6],\n 5: [24, 8, 7],\n 6: [13, 11, 8, 7],\n 7: [13, 11, 8],\n 8: [12],\n 9: [10, 10]}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe two groups overlap. Seven students of Les Amis de l'ABC and Gavroche are in both,\nwith Mabeuf and Marius completing one group and Grantaire and Prouvaire the other:\n\n::: {#d76ccabd .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfirst, second = map(set, components[9])\ngroup = {n: \"with Marius\" for n in first}\ngroup |= {n: \"with Grantaire\" for n in second}\ngroup |= {n: \"both\" for n in first & second}\n\nmiserables.set_node_attrs(\"k9_group\", group)\nmiserables.subgraph(list(first | second)).layout_kamada_kawai().plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"k9_group\", label_by=\"label\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-co